/testbed/docs/utils/iers.rst:The first time that one attempts a time or coordinate transformation that
/testbed/docs/utils/iers.rst-requires IERS data, the latest version of the IERS table (from 1973 through
/testbed/docs/utils/iers.rst-one year into the future) will be downloaded and stored in the astropy cache.
/testbed/docs/utils/iers.rst-
/testbed/docs/utils/iers.rst-Transformations will then use the cached data file if possible.  However, the
/testbed/docs/utils/iers.rst-``IERS_Auto`` table is automatically updated in place from the network if the
/testbed/docs/utils/iers.rst-following two conditions a met when the table is queried for ``UT1-UTC`` or
/testbed/docs/utils/iers.rst-polar motion values:
/testbed/docs/utils/iers.rst-
/testbed/docs/utils/iers.rst-- Any of the requested IERS values are *predictive*, meaning that they have
/testbed/docs/utils/iers.rst-  been extrapolated into the future with a model that is fit to measured data.
--
/testbed/docs/utils/iers.rst:- The first predictive values in the table are at least ``conf.auto_max_age
/testbed/docs/utils/iers.rst-  days`` old relative to the current actual time (i.e. ``Time.now()``).  This
/testbed/docs/utils/iers.rst-  means that the IERS table is out of date and a newer version can be found on
/testbed/docs/utils/iers.rst-  the IERS service.
/testbed/docs/utils/iers.rst-
/testbed/docs/utils/iers.rst-The IERS Service provides the default online table
/testbed/docs/utils/iers.rst-(set by ``astropy.utils.iers.IERS_A_URL``) and updates the content
/testbed/docs/utils/iers.rst-once each 7 days.  The default value of ``auto_max_age`` is 30 days to avoid
/testbed/docs/utils/iers.rst-unnecessary network access, but one can reduce this to as low as 10 days.
