/testbed/docs/io/fits/usage/unfamiliar.rst:FITS binary tables. The principle used in this convention is to first divide
/testbed/docs/io/fits/usage/unfamiliar.rst-the n-dimensional image into a rectangular grid of sub-images or 'tiles'.
/testbed/docs/io/fits/usage/unfamiliar.rst-Each tile is then compressed as a continuous block of data, and the resulting
/testbed/docs/io/fits/usage/unfamiliar.rst-compressed byte stream is stored in a row of a variable length column in a
/testbed/docs/io/fits/usage/unfamiliar.rst-FITS binary table. Several commonly used algorithms for compressing image
/testbed/docs/io/fits/usage/unfamiliar.rst-tiles are supported. These include Gzip, Rice, IRAF Pixel List (PLIO), and
/testbed/docs/io/fits/usage/unfamiliar.rst-Hcompress.
/testbed/docs/io/fits/usage/unfamiliar.rst-
/testbed/docs/io/fits/usage/unfamiliar.rst-For more details, reference "A FITS Image Compression Proposal" from:
/testbed/docs/io/fits/usage/unfamiliar.rst-
/testbed/docs/io/fits/usage/unfamiliar.rst-    https://www.adass.org/adass/proceedings/adass99/P2-42/
